24	/**
25	 * REAL INCIDENTS — docs/QA-PRELAUNCH.md P0-6 (and its mirror images):
26	 *
27	 *  1. Upgrade proration priced the RAW list-price monthly delta while the original
28	 *     order applied both the term discount and the seasonal promo. On a 36-month
29	 *     term with the 30% Summer Sale running, upgrading Starter -> Max the day after
30	 *     purchase cost $1,044.45 against $665.08 for buying Max outright on the same
31	 *     end date: the customer overpaid $379.37.
32	 *  2. The downgrade CREDIT used that same undiscounted delta, so it refunded more
33	 *     than had ever been paid — a self-service money printer that was one
34	 *     cheap-but-storage-heavy plan away from reachable (blocked today only by the
35	 *     disk-monotonic guard, QA P1-14).
36	 *  3. The fix for (2) also CAPPED the credit's duration at one term while the
37	 *     charge side kept pricing every remaining month, which broke the round trip in
38	 *     the other direction. A customer whose stacked "Renew now" had pushed
39	 *     `expiresAt` 36 months out on a 12-month term paid $544.32 to go
40	 *     Starter -> Max and got $181.44 back coming straight down: the platform kept
41	 *     $362.88 of months they had paid for. `remainingMonths` no longer takes a cap
42	 *     at all, so the two directions cannot express different durations.
43	 *  4. PROMO-WINDOW ARBITRAGE (2026-07-26). The rate basis for BOTH directions
44	 *     defaulted to `currentPromo(now)` — the seasonal promo running at the instant
45	 *     the resize was priced. Promos rotate by calendar date from a 15% Launch Sale
46	 *     up to 45% for Cyber Monday, and lib/promotions.ts publishes the timetable. So
47	 *     the customer picked the price of each half by picking the day: upgrade
48	 *     Starter -> Max on 1 December (delta charged at 55% of the honest rate) and
49	 *     downgrade back on 1 October (same delta credited at 85%) and keep the spread
50	 *     on every remaining month — $207.36 on a 36-month term, repeatable every year,
51	 *     with no net change to the server. Both directions now price against
52	 *     `Server.termPromoPercent`, the rate the term was ACTUALLY sold at, persisted
53	 *     at every point a term is bought or renewed. The date is no longer an input to
54	 *     this module at all.
55	 *
56	 * Both directions price through lib/proration.ts. These tests hold the two halves
57	 * against each other so they cannot drift apart again.
58	 *
59	 * Deterministic: every `now` and every rate is injected — nothing reads the clock
60	 * except through a value supplied by the test.
61	 */
